00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.300
Donald Trump won the election in 2016 vowing to build a wall to deport all unauthorized residents

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:11.160
and to massively reduce the number of people welcomed here legally.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:17.580
COVID-19, which has its origins in Wuhan China, may help the President to deliver fully

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:24.480
on those campaign promises. Is the mythology of America as a nation of immigrants coming to an end?

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:32.080
I can't point to an incident, in American history at least, where financial and economic collapse led to

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.960
a Kumbaya feeling, where everyone is like oh you're different from me that's okay.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:43.720
A deputy national editor at the New York Times, Jia Lynn Yang, is the author of the timely new book,

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:52.260
"One Mighty and Irresistible Tide: The Epic Struggle over American Immigration, 1924 to 1965.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.660
The book begins at another dark moment in American immigration policy

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:03.240
when a restrictive law ended a long period of relatively open borders and effectively stopped mass movement

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:09.780
to the United States. It tells the story of the decades-long battle that led the US to begin accepting foreigners

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:16.540
once again, and yet nobody involved in that fight foresaw the extent to which the 1965 law,

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:21.700
signed by President Lyndon Johnson, would open the door once again to new immigrants,

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.140
including Yang's family, which came here from Taiwan in the 1970's.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:35.240
Our beautiful America was built by a nation of strangers. From a hundred different places or more

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:45.980
they have poured forth into an empty land, joining and blending in one mighty and irresistible tide.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:50.340
I sat down with Yang in our studio in early March and then more recently in May

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:55.620
to discuss what her book and personal story can teach us about the current moment.

